    <string name="marimo_tip_body_1">Keep marimos in cool water (15–25°C). Avoid direct sunlight and warm spots.</string>
    <string name="marimo_tip_body_2">Squeeze and roll your marimo gently to maintain its round shape.</string>
    <string name="marimo_tip_body_3">Use filtered or distilled water. Chlorinated water can harm marimos.</string>
    <string name="marimo_tip_body_4">Marimos prefer indirect light. Too much sun makes them brown.</string>
    <string name="marimo_tip_body_5">Don\'t overcrowd containers. Each marimo needs space to move freely.</string>
    <string name="marimo_tip_body_6">Healthy marimos make tiny oxygen bubbles - this is a great sign!</string>
    <string name="marimo_tip_body_7">In winter, marimos grow slower and need less frequent water changes.</string>
    <string name="marimo_tip_body_8">Marimos grow only 5mm per year. Patience is key with these friends!</string>
    <string name="marimo_tip_body_9">Large marimos can be gently split into smaller ones to share.</string>
    <string name="marimo_tip_body_10">Swirl the water occasionally to help marimos move and stay healthy.</string>
